#31033
NAME   In Front of a Hermits House~
DESCR
This small cottage looks to be made of boulders from the mountain nearby, light gray and ireregularly shpaed, but somehow cemented together perfectly at each seam.  It looks as if the rocks just melted together where they were touching.  The cottage seems to lean slightly to the left as you look on it, with a small broken window on the right side, and the left side of the cottage has an old wooden door that looks sturdy, but aged with time.  The handle is smooth from use, but worn from the weather.  The ceiling of this cottage looks to be made of grass and dirt, with short green grasses covering the entirety.  The chimney towards the back has fallen over, with some of the stones still on the roof, and others on the ground nearby.
~
FLAGS  0
Sect   2
DOOR 1
You see nothing special here.
~
~
0 0 31035
DOOR 2
You see nothing special here.
~
~
0 0 31034
DOOR 3
You see nothing special here.
~
~
0 0 31028
EDESC boulders rocks~
The boulders making up the walls of this cottage are oddly rough in their general shape for how smoothly they are fit together with one another.  Where two boulders touch together it almost looks as if they melted to fit snugly with one another, but the rest is a normal rough hewn boulder that looks to have been unearthed directly from the mountain.  ~
EDESC window pane~
The window is made with a few pieces of wood acting as framing for the smooth glass windows.  One of the window panes in the four paned window seems to have had a large hole knocked through it, though some of the glass pieces still remain in that quadrant.  Each window pane quadrant is about the size of someone's head, allowing plenty of light to enter from the west.~
EDESC door handle~
The door of this cottage looks like normal wood at first, but as you look closer you see rivets of a starange silvery metal in the hinges.  The handle is made of wood, as are the panels of the door, but they are fit together snugly and oiled well enough that they do not seem to have been weathered like some of the other areas of the cottage.  You notice that the handle has a place for a lock, but instead of having one there is just a hole through the door where it should have been.~
EDESC hole peephole lock~
Putting your eye to the lock-hole, you can see clear through into the cottage
beyond.  A young woman moves about a warm little kitchen, humming to herself as
she stirs a pot over the fire, wholly unaware that anyone is watching.  The
bright silvery rivets around the hole frame the scene like a picture -- worked
from that same untarnished metal you keep finding wherever this strange little
life has left its mark.~
End

